WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.340
Dry, grade, pack and prove it. Adding value after harvest.

2
00:00:04.690 --> 00:00:09.040
This is the video for module two of Agribusiness and Market Access.

3
00:00:10.290 --> 00:00:21.210
Most of the extra money a farmer can earn is won or lost in the days after harvest. Done carelessly, handling causes mould, insects, bruising and weight loss.

4
00:00:22.460 --> 00:00:30.610
Done well, it gives you produce buyers trust and pay more for. And simple records prove whether your changes really raised your income.

5
00:00:31.860 --> 00:00:47.820
Wet grain is the root of most storage problems. For maize, about 13% moisture or lower is usually recommended for safe storage. Dry on a clean tarpaulin, never on bare earth or the roadside, and turn it several times a day.

6
00:00:48.170 --> 00:00:54.040
To test, fill a clean, dry jar about one third with grain from several places in the heap.

7
00:00:55.290 --> 00:00:58.470
Add two to three tablespoons of dry table salt.

8
00:00:59.720 --> 00:01:03.960
Close it, shake for about two minutes, and let it rest for about fifteen minutes.

9
00:01:05.210 --> 00:01:06.470
Shake again and look.

10
00:01:07.720 --> 00:01:17.110
If salt sticks to the inside, the grain is still too wet. Keep drying and test again. If the salt falls freely, it is dry enough in most cases.

11
00:01:18.360 --> 00:01:28.450
Grading means sorting produce into groups of similar quality, so a buyer knows exactly what she is getting. Here is Grace. These prices are illustrative.

12
00:01:29.700 --> 00:01:33.690
Sold mixed, her 30 crates bring 420,000 naira.

13
00:01:34.940 --> 00:01:45.520
She sorts them: 18 crates of firm Grade A, 9 of Grade B, and 3 soft crates for soup sellers. Together they bring 459,000 naira.

14
00:01:46.770 --> 00:01:49.350
She pays two helpers 4,000 naira each.

15
00:01:50.600 --> 00:01:57.340
Her net gain is 31,000 naira on one load. And the soft fruit no longer spoils the good fruit.

16
00:01:58.590 --> 00:02:12.050
Now safety. Aflatoxins are poisons from moulds on maize, groundnuts and other grains and nuts. Long-term exposure is linked to liver cancer, and normal cooking does not reliably destroy them.

17
00:02:12.400 --> 00:02:18.820
Harvest on time, dry fast, remove mouldy grain, and never sell it, eat it or feed it to animals.

18
00:02:20.070 --> 00:02:33.660
Every pesticide label states the pre-harvest interval, the days you must wait between the last spray and harvest. Wear protection, never spray into the wind, and never store chemicals in food or drink containers.

19
00:02:34.910 --> 00:02:46.710
Never keep grain storage tablets such as aluminium phosphide in or near your home, and never use them yourself. Use hermetic bags or methods your extension officer advises.

20
00:02:47.960 --> 00:02:58.560
Overfilled raffia baskets crush the fruit at the bottom. Halima lost about 20% of her loads that way. A group member using crates loses about 5%.

21
00:02:59.810 --> 00:03:06.260
That saves 6 loads a season. At about 15,000 naira a load, that is 90,000 naira.

22
00:03:07.510 --> 00:03:16.060
Twenty crates cost about 100,000 naira, so they pay for themselves in just over one season, and she reuses them for several more.

23
00:03:17.310 --> 00:03:40.120
Buyers quote in bags, baskets and mudu, so divide every price by the weight in kilograms. Ngozi's offer of 62,000 naira for a 100 kg bag is 620 naira a kilo. 32,500 naira for a 50 kg bag sounds smaller, but it is 650 naira a kilo, the better deal.

24
00:03:41.370 --> 00:03:51.030
Write each sale the same day: date, buyer, kilos, price per kilo, selling costs and net amount. Google Sheets on your phone can do the arithmetic.

25
00:03:52.280 --> 00:03:57.080
Change one thing at a time, and compare your net price per kilo before and after.

26
00:03:58.330 --> 00:04:07.230
Check your own bank or mobile money app, because SMS alerts and screenshots can be faked. Keep your PIN and one-time codes private.

27
00:04:08.480 --> 00:04:23.260
To remember. Dry grain on a tarpaulin and test it before storing. Grade so your best produce earns more. Never sell or eat mouldy grain. Respect the pre-harvest interval on every label. And compare net naira per kilogram.

28
00:04:24.510 --> 00:04:38.800
Now practise. Dry, test and grade one batch, and label each grade. Ask a buyer for their written specification and prepare one lot to meet it. Then set up your sales record and write your baseline price per kilo.

29
00:04:39.150 --> 00:04:41.090
Continue with the lessons below.
